course: biology
lesson: 47
title: Who eats whom: predators, prey, scavengers and decomposers
level: primary-4
stars: 2
explain: A predator hunts and kills other animals for food, its prey; scavengers eat animals that are already dead, and decomposers such as fungi, worms and dung beetles break down dead things and dung so the goodness goes back to the soil.
explain: When your well-fed pet cat drops a mouse on the kitchen floor, it is not hungry and not giving you a present on purpose: the urge to stalk and pounce is built into every cat.
explain: Prey animals survive by spotting danger, hiding, copying dangerous animals or even playing dead, and predators answer with sharp eyes, speed and teamwork, a race that never ends.
explain: Cartoons make the hyena a cowardly thief, the vulture a villain and the fly-eating plant a monster; in real life hyenas hunt most of their own food, vultures keep the land clean, and the Venus flytrap is a small plant on a poor, wet patch of ground.
fact: Cats are predators: even well-fed pet cats hunt mice, birds and insects, and pet cats in Britain have been estimated to catch tens of millions of animals a year.
fact: A kestrel hovers into the wind with its head perfectly still, which keeps the picture steady, to spot the tiny movements of voles and mice in the grass; a 1995 study suggested it may see ultraviolet light from voles' urine trails.
fact: Prey such as rabbits and deer have eyes on the sides of the head to see almost all the way round; predators such as foxes, owls and cats have eyes facing forward to judge distance before a pounce.
fact: The best-supported idea about zebra stripes today is that they put off biting flies such as horseflies, which fail to land properly on stripes; the idea that they confuse lions has little proof.
fact: Vultures are scavengers whose very strong stomach acid kills most germs from rotting meat; when vultures nearly died out in India after eating cattle treated with a medicine poisonous to them, carcasses lay uncleaned and disease spread.
fact: It is a myth that hyenas only steal from lions: spotted hyenas hunt most of their own food in many places, lions often steal from them, and they are closer relatives of cats and mongooses than of dogs.
fact: Dung beetles roll dung into balls and bury them as food for their young; some steer at night by the glow of the Milky Way, and Australia brought in dung beetles to clear up cow pats, which had piled up in the fields and bred flies.
fact: Earthworms pull dead leaves underground and turn them into rich worm casts, and their tunnels let air and water into the soil; Charles Darwin wrote a whole book about them in 1881.
fact: Fungi are decomposers whose thin threads break down dead wood, and mushrooms are only their fruiting bodies; many wild mushrooms are deadly, so never touch or eat one you cannot name.
fact: A hoverfly has wasp-like yellow and black stripes but cannot sting; copying a dangerous animal's look to fool predators is called mimicry.
fact: A Virginia opossum chased by a predator may fall over, go stiff, drool and smell bad for minutes or hours, because many predators lose interest in prey that seems dead.
fact: A Venus flytrap is a plant predator that snaps its leaves shut on insects; it grows in poor soil and gets extra food from insects; its trigger hairs must be touched twice within about 20 seconds, so a raindrop or a speck of dust does not set it off, and it grows wild only in a small area of North and South Carolina.
exercise: What is a predator? | An animal that hunts and kills other animals for food | An animal that eats only plants and never touches meat at all | An animal that eats only dead animals | A living thing that breaks down dead leaves
exercise: Why does a well-fed pet cat still catch mice? | The urge to stalk and pounce is built into cats | It is starving because cat food has no energy | It wants to give its owner a present | Mice attack cats first
exercise: Why does a kestrel keep its head perfectly still while hovering? | A still head keeps the picture steady so it can spot tiny movements | It is resting its tired neck muscles after a long flight over the field | It is listening for mice with its feet | It is asleep in the air
exercise: Why are a rabbit's eyes on the sides of its head? | To see danger coming from almost any direction | To judge distance well before a long jump | To look at two carrots at once | Because rabbits cannot turn their heads round at all
exercise: Why are a fox's eyes on the front of its head? | Both eyes overlap to judge distance before a pounce | To see what is happening behind itself | To keep dust out of them | So it can see right down to the bottom of rabbit holes
exercise: What is the best-supported reason for zebra stripes today? | They put off biting flies | They make zebras invisible in long grass | They confuse lions so they never catch zebras | They keep zebras warm at night
exercise: What do vultures eat? | Animals that are already dead | Only live animals they catch themselves | Only fruit and seeds they find on trees | Only insects they snatch out of the air
exercise: How can vultures eat rotting meat without getting ill? | Their very strong stomach acid kills most germs | They leave it to cook in the hot sun for a few days first | They only eat the bones | They take medicine from farmers
exercise: Do spotted hyenas only steal other animals' food? | No, they hunt most of their own food, and lions often steal from them | Yes, they never hunt at all and live only on what they can steal | Yes, they only eat what lions leave | No, they only eat plants
exercise: What does a dung beetle do with a ball of dung? | Buries it as food for itself and its young | Uses it as a football to play with other beetles | Builds a house out of it | Throws it at birds
exercise: How do earthworms help the soil? | They pull dead leaves down and their tunnels let in air and water | They eat all the roots of the plants | They make the soil dry and hard | They keep the soil clean and free of all other living things, even germs
exercise: What do fungi do to a rotting log? | Their threads break it down and return its goodness to the soil | They glue the broken log back together | They make it grow new branches | They protect it from rotting
exercise: Why do birds leave a hoverfly alone? | It looks like a stinging wasp, though it cannot sting | It tastes of sugar and makes birds sick | It flies faster than any bird in the sky can fly | It is too small to see
exercise: What is it called when a harmless animal copies a dangerous one's look? | Mimicry | Camouflage | Migration | Hibernation
exercise: Why does an opossum play dead? | Many predators lose interest in prey that seems dead | It is really fainting from tiredness after running away | It is taking a nap | It wants to be carried home by the dog
exercise: Why does a Venus flytrap need two touches before it shuts? | So a raindrop or speck of dust does not set it off | Because it is half asleep | Because the first touch only says hello to the insect and wakes it up | So it can count the flies
exercise: Why does a Venus flytrap catch insects at all? | It grows in poor soil and gets extra food from insects | It has no leaves to make food | It does it to protect other plants | It eats only insects and never uses sunlight like other plants do
test: In a cartoon the hyenas are lazy thieves who never hunt. What is the truth? | Spotted hyenas hunt most of their own food, and lions often steal from them | Hyenas never hunt anything | Hyenas only eat grass | Hyenas are a kind of wild dog that follows lions about and begs for leftovers
test: «vultures are gross, would be better if they all died» What is the clever answer? | Without them carcasses rot and spread disease, as happened in India | Right, nothing would change | Right, other birds would just eat more fruit and seeds instead | Vultures spread disease, so the fewer of them there are, the better
test: «ugh cat brought in another mouse, she's not even hungry» What is right? | Hunting is built into cats, hungry or not | She is secretly starving, cat food is empty | She is training to be a dog, like the one next door | She caught it only to make friends with it and play
test: A zebra herd stands at a waterhole full of horseflies. What does the best idea about stripes predict? | Fewer horseflies land properly on the zebras | The flies cannot see the zebras at all, so they bite the lions | The zebras will turn black | The stripes attract more flies
test: What would happen to a field full of cow pats if there were no dung beetles or other decomposers? | The dung would pile up and flies would breed in it | The dung would turn into fresh green grass by itself | The cows would stop making dung | Nothing, dung just vanishes
test: «that's a wasp, swat it!» — but it hovers still in the air with one pair of wings. What is it? | Probably a harmless hoverfly | A wasp that has lost its sting | A baby bee that has not grown up yet | A bumblebee in disguise, waiting to sting you
test: In a horror film a giant flytrap eats people. What is true about a real Venus flytrap? | It is a small plant catching insects in a few wild places in the Carolinas | It grows big enough to eat dogs | It catches insects with long roots that reach up out of the ground and grab them | It lives in every garden in Europe
test: «found a cool mushroom on the log, gonna eat it, looks fine» What is the best advice? | Never eat a wild mushroom you cannot name; many are deadly | Fine, mushrooms that grow on logs are always safe to eat | Fine, just cook it first | Only eat the white ones
test: «the dog killed the opossum, it's lying there with its tongue out» What could be going on? | It may be playing dead and get up when the dog leaves | It is definitely dead | It is sleeping because it is tired | It is cooling down by panting with its tongue out, like a dog
test: A rabbit and a fox stare at each other across a field. Who sees more of the field around them, and who judges distance better? | The rabbit sees more around; the fox judges distance better | The fox sees more around; the rabbit judges distance better | They both see exactly the same | Neither can see the other
test: «worms are useless, I'd chuck 'em all out of the garden» What would happen? | Dead leaves would pile up and the soil would get poorer | The plants would grow twice as fast | Nothing at all would change | The soil would fill with more air and water, and everything would grow better
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a scavenger? | An animal which subsists upon the bodies of animals already dead | An animal which hunts and kills living prey exclusively, by day and by night | An organism which manufactures its own food from sunlight | An animal which subsists exclusively upon plants
exam: Identify the principal advantage conferred upon prey animals by eyes situated upon the sides of the head. | A field of vision almost all round, so that danger may be perceived from any side | Precise judgement of distance prior to a pounce | Improved vision in conditions of total darkness | Protection of the eyes from the attacks of predators, which strike chiefly at the front
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the hoverfly. | It bears a wasp's colouring but cannot sting, an instance of mimicry | It is a species of wasp possessing an exceptionally powerful sting | It is a bee which has lost its colouring | It is a beetle which hovers by means of four wings
exam: Ascertain the consequence which ensued upon the near disappearance of vultures in India. | Carcasses remained uncleaned and disease was observed to spread | The number of cattle declined sharply | Crops ceased to grow throughout the region | Hyenas were introduced from Africa in great numbers to replace them
